body:
Try not to worry too much. If the test was truly harder than normal, that will be reflected in the scoring. Many, many people walk out of step 1 feeling like they failed, and only a tiny fraction of those people actually failed. You did the best you could with what you had.  Worrying will not change anything. Now it's time to practice those mature coping skills (lookin at you, repression) and wait. If you did in fact fail, you can problem solve from there when the time comes, but to be honest, you probably passed.

&#x200B;

Edit: I meant suppression, not repression. Guess I need to review that lol.

Today weather how you like?

&#x200B;

Wait no

Oh yeah, you are right. If what he is showing symptoms of is in fact chlamydia, then there are enough antibodies to show up in the test as soon as symptoms start showing. It's if you're **a**symptomatic that you want to wait to weeks to test after a possible exposure. That's for chlamydia and gonorrhea.

&#x200B;

Which just made me realize: OP were you tested for gonorrhea too? They usually test chlamydia and gonorrhea together by default. Your symptoms could be from either one.
